    <h2>4. System Design &amp; Academic UML Models</h2>

    <h3>4.1 Use Case Diagram</h3>
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    Patient((Patient))
    Doctor((Doctor))
    Admin((Administrator))

    subgraph CareConnect360 Boundary
        UC1[Register & Login Account]
        UC2[Search & Book Doctor Slot]
        UC3[Join WebRTC Video Consultation]
        UC4[Mute/Cam Toggle & Screen Share]
        UC5[Upload Diagnostic Reports & Vitals]
        UC6[Conduct Video OPD Consultation]
        UC7[Author e-Prescription with CDSS Alerts]
        UC8[Digitally Sign & Issue Prescription]
        UC9[Download Verifiable PDF Prescription]
        UC10[Audit Platform Logs & User Moderation]
    end

    Patient --> UC1
    Patient --> UC2
    Patient --> UC3
    Patient --> UC4
    Patient --> UC5
    Patient --> UC9

    Doctor --> UC1
    Doctor --> UC3
    Doctor --> UC4
    Doctor --> UC6
    Doctor --> UC7
    Doctor --> UC8

    Admin --> UC1
    Admin --> UC10
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-caption">Figure 4.1: Use Case Diagram for Patient, Doctor, and Administrator Workflows</div>
    </div>

    <h3>4.2 Data Flow Diagram (DFD Level 0 - System Context)</h3>
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    Patient["Patient Actor"]
    Doctor["Doctor / Specialist"]
    System[["CareConnect360 Telemedicine Platform"]]
    EHR[("Health Records & DB Repository")]

    Patient -->|Login, Slot Booking, AV Stream, Vitals| System
    System -->|Booking Confirmation, Remote Video, Signed PDF| Patient

    Doctor -->|Availability, Diagnosis, Digital Signature| System
    System -->|Patient Queue, Incoming Video Call, Medical History| Doctor

    System <-->|Read / Write Patient, Appointments & Prescription Records| EHR
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-caption">Figure 4.2: Data Flow Diagram (DFD Level 0 Context Model)</div>
    </div>

    <h3>4.3 Entity-Relationship (ER) Schema Model</h3>
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <div class="mermaid">
erDiagram
    USERS {
        string user_id PK
        string full_name
        string email UK
        string password_hash
        string role
        string phone
    }

    DOCTORS {
        string doctor_id PK
        string user_id FK
        string specialty
        string qualification
        string registration_no
        string hospital_clinic
        decimal consultation_fee
    }

    APPOINTMENTS {
        string appointment_id PK
        string patient_id FK
        string doctor_id FK
        date appointment_date
        string time_slot
        string status
        string chief_complaint
    }

    PRESCRIPTIONS {
        string prescription_id PK
        string appointment_id FK
        string doctor_id FK
        string patient_id FK
        string diagnosis
        json medications_list
        string digital_signature_hash
    }

    USERS ||--o| DOCTORS : "has profile"
    USERS ||--o{ APPOINTMENTS : "books as patient"
    DOCTORS ||--o{ APPOINTMENTS : "attends as doctor"
    APPOINTMENTS ||--o| PRESCRIPTIONS : "generates"
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-caption">Figure 4.3: Entity-Relationship Diagram in 3rd Normal Form (3NF)</div>
    </div>

    <h3>4.4 WebRTC Peer-to-Peer Sequence Architecture</h3>
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Patient as Patient (Browser Tab 1)
    participant Signaling as Express / PeerJS Server (:5500)
    actor Doctor as Doctor (Browser Tab 2)

    Patient->>Patient: Request getUserMedia (Webcam/Mic)
    Doctor->>Doctor: Request getUserMedia (Webcam/Mic)
    
    Patient->>Signaling: Connect with Peer ID "cc_room_patient"
    Doctor->>Signaling: Connect with Peer ID "cc_room_doctor"
    
    Signaling-->>Patient: Connection Established
    Signaling-->>Doctor: Connection Established
    
    Patient->>Signaling: Dispatch Call Offer (SDP + Audio/Video Stream)
    Signaling->>Doctor: Forward Call Offer from Patient
    
    Doctor->>Doctor: Answer Call with Local Media Stream
    Doctor->>Signaling: Dispatch SDP Answer
    Signaling->>Patient: Deliver SDP Answer
    
    Note over Patient,Doctor: Direct P2P DTLS-SRTP Media Channel Open
    Patient<<-->>Doctor: 2-Way HD Video, Audio, and Screen Sharing
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-caption">Figure 4.4: WebRTC Signaling and Direct Media Channel Sequence Diagram</div>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. Non-Functional Requirements -->
    <h2>5. Non-Functional Requirements (NFRs)</h2>
    <table class="content-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="width: 22%;">Attribute</th>
          <th style="width: 38%;">Target Benchmark</th>
          <th>Testing &amp; Verification Protocol</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5.1 Performance</strong></td>
          <td>Initial load &lt; 1.8s; WebRTC stream latency &lt; 200ms; REST API latency &lt; 150ms.</td>
          <td>JMeter load testing under 50 concurrent simulated users.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5.2 Security</strong></td>
          <td>Salted Bcrypt password hashing (10 rounds); DTLS-SRTP 256-bit media stream encryption.</td>
          <td>OWASP ZAP vulnerability scanning and automated penetration testing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5.3 Reliability</strong></td>
          <td>Uptime &ge; 99.0%; automatic session recovery on ICE candidate timeout.</td>
          <td>Stress testing with simulated network drops.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5.4 Portability</strong></td>
          <td>Chrome (v100+), Edge (v100+), Firefox (v98+), Safari (v15+); responsive mobile viewports.</td>
          <td>Cross-browser testing across mobile and desktop display viewports.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

